      "text": "To determine the probability that exactly two out of five 12-sided dice show an even number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the probability of rolling an even number on a single die:**\n   Each die has 12 faces numbered from 1 to 12. The even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12, which are 6 in total. Therefore, the probability \\( P(E) \\) of rolling an even number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(E) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   The probability of rolling an odd number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(O) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose which 2 dice out of the 5 will show an even number:**\n   This is a combination problem where we choose 2 dice out of 5. The number of ways to do this is given by the binomial coefficient:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability of a specific combination of 2 even and 3 odd outcomes:**\n   The probability of a specific combination of 2 even and 3 odd outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{2 even and 3 odd}) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the number of ways to choose the dice with even numbers and the probability of each combination:**\n   The total probability is the product of the number of ways to choose the dice and the probability of each combination:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{exactly 2 even}) = \\binom{5}{2} \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\\).",
