      "text": "We have a 12-sided die with faces numbered from 1 to 12. The total number of outcomes when rolling 5 such dice is \\(12^5\\), since each die has 12 possible outcomes.\n\nNext, we need to determine the number of ways to get exactly 2 evens. First, we calculate the probability of getting an even number on a single die. For a 12-sided die, half the numbers are even (2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12), so the probability \\(p\\) of getting an even number on a single roll is \\(\\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSimilarly, the probability of getting an odd number is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\). We need to choose 2 dice to show even numbers out of the 5 dice. The number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5 is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{5}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nFor the 2 chosen dice, each must show an even number. The probability that each of these 2 dice shows an even number is \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{4}\\). The probability that the other 3 dice show odd numbers is \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{8}\\). Therefore, the probability that exactly 2 dice show even numbers is:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{8} = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\n\\]",
